WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:05.180
Dany and Drogon's dramatic
rescue of Jon Snow

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:08.030
and his fellow warriors is one
of the most memorable scenes

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:10.130
from season seven of "Game of Thrones."

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:11.910
But before that scene came to life,

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:15.063
it started here, in a
slightly less polished place.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:18.390
Before filming ever began,

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.070
certain problems had to be solved,

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.360
like how all the characters
would fit on the dragon

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.830
and what they'd hold to avoid falling off.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:27.170
Also, what does a dragon even look like?

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:29.972
Building these mythical
creatures from scratch required

00:00:29.972 --> 00:00:32.772
a combination of elements
found in the natural world,

00:00:32.772 --> 00:00:34.040
like the scales of a lizard,

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.281
the wings of a bat, and
the neck of a T. rex,

00:00:36.281 --> 00:00:38.043
all to create this.

00:00:38.043 --> 00:00:39.501
(dragon roaring)

00:00:41.451 --> 00:00:43.192
Dan Katcher: I built that gigantic thing

00:00:43.192 --> 00:00:45.367
in this little, tiny bedroom.

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:46.700
Narrator: This is Dan Katcher,

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.660
the self-proclaimed "father of dragons,"

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:50.470
and he's the man responsible for designing

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:52.780
these iconic creatures for
one of the biggest shows

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:54.241
in television history.

00:00:59.371 --> 00:01:02.510
(dragon roaring)

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.780
The "Game of Thrones"
dragons set a new standard

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:07.380
for how these creatures are
designed and brought to life.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.643
Katcher: For me, every season
it would go anywhere from

00:01:09.643 --> 00:01:12.243
eight weeks to 12 weeks

00:01:12.243 --> 00:01:15.743
of actually working and
designing these guys.

00:01:15.743 --> 00:01:17.110
Narrator: It all starts with a sketch,

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.730
like this early drawing of Drogon.

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.660
But it's not just about sketching.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.810
Katcher creates detailed models

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.740
that he sends to HBO for approval,

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.520
models that have to take into account

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.233
the proportions of the dragons...

00:01:28.233 --> 00:01:30.070
(dragon screeching)

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:33.330
which grew from tiny to massive
during the run of the show,

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.400
how the creatures would
work inside and out,

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.380
and every single detail down
to the individual scales.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.120
To do this, Katcher used
a program called ZBrush.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.720
The first step for creating
one of his 3D models

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.930
is creating the proportions
of the creatures

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:49.003
using elements called ZSpheres.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:52.720
Before he can think about
how the creature will look,

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.920
he has to create the basic infrastructure

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.023
for how it will work.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.233
Pretty soon, you see the basic
building blocks of a dragon.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:05.720
Katcher then creates an
internal structure, or skeleton.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.910
He lays out bones that will
be connected to muscle,

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:09.550
establishing the physical dynamics

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:12.490
of how this creature will
move and, eventually, fly.

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:14.670
An understanding of physiology and anatomy

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.620
is crucial to Katcher's designs,

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.290
many of which are inspired
by real-life animals.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:20.690
Katcher: In the skeleton
itself, I've mixed

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:21.670
all these animals together.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.290
So, you've got bat wings
that are in the bones.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:24.930
You've got a bird rib cage.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.430
These are neck vertebrae taken from a

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:27.720
Tyrannosaurus rex.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.540
Narrator: For his dragon, Katcher combined

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.430
specific elements of real animals;

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:33.410
the scales on the dragon's head look like

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:34.880
those of a sungazer lizard.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.826
And you'll notice the similarities between

00:02:36.826 --> 00:02:38.358
the scales on its chest

00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:40.870
and the feathers on the chest of an eagle.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:42.710
Katcher puts his inspiration to work

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:45.140
in the most time-consuming
part of the process:

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.550
creating and refining
the extremely detailed

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:49.360
features of the dragon's anatomy,

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:51.150
like its teeth and its scales.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:53.160
Although ZBrush has
tools that allow artists

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.950
to repeat the patterns of
the scales, thus saving time,

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:59.090
Katcher meticulously
designs every scale himself

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:01.440
to avoid the appearance of artificiality.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.682
Once the detailed model
is complete in ZBrush,

00:03:03.682 --> 00:03:07.140
Katcher exports a 2D image
to perform a paint-over

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.880
for an image that's submitted
to producers for approval.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.000
But the initial design
is only the beginning.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.680
Katcher would be given the story line,

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.670
which would determine
specific modifications

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:17.833
that needed to be made for each dragon.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.590
Like in that scene in season seven.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:21.651
Katcher: So I had to
show, like, all right,

00:03:21.651 --> 00:03:23.035
well here's how all the guys

00:03:23.035 --> 00:03:25.335
can hold on to the scales on Drogon's back

00:03:25.335 --> 00:03:26.410
to eventually be pulled away.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:28.720
So that's, like, part
of the design process.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.670
Narrator: Katcher exports
his files from ZBrush

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:32.430
and sends them to
visual-effects companies,

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.103
who import the files into programs

00:03:34.103 --> 00:03:36.005
like Maya and 3D Studio Max

00:03:36.005 --> 00:03:37.988
to begin rigging and
animating the creatures

00:03:37.988 --> 00:03:39.546
that you see come to life on screen.

00:03:39.546 --> 00:03:41.600
Katcher: The technology now
is at the point where, really,

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.863
if you could conceive
of it, you can make it.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:48.200
Narrator: So, how do you
get a job designing dragons?

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:49.541
Katcher: I was working on a show,

00:03:49.541 --> 00:03:51.270
called "Terra Nova," with dinosaurs,

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.480
and a gentleman by the name
of Rainer Gombos came in,

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.150
who happened to be the executive producer

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:56.490
for the visual effects
for "Game of Thrones,"

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:58.380
and he was looking for a dragon.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.440
And it just so happened
that I had spent my life

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:04.010
making dinosaurs and dragons,
like for McFarlane Toys.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:05.017
Narrator: Years before getting hired

00:04:05.017 --> 00:04:07.560
on to "Game of Thrones,"
Katcher worked as a toy maker,

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.350
designing and sculpting creature figurines

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:11.390
for "Spawn" creator Todd McFarlane's

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:12.870
New York-based toy company.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.520
Katcher: I had already
had a pretty big portfolio

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.080
of different dragon works to show them,

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.350
so I didn't have to do another
concept on top of that.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:19.720
They just sort of looked at my work,

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:20.818
and Rainer trusted me and said,

00:04:20.818 --> 00:04:23.097
"We see you know what you're
doing, just go for it."

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.850
So that design was always in me,

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:29.480
and finally "Game of Thrones"
just let it come out.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:30.810
Narrator: Katcher
received Emmy recognition

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:32.110
for his work on "Game of Thrones."

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:34.640
Today, he is busy creating
creatures for DC shows

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.724
like "The Flash,"
"Titans," and "Supergirl."

00:04:37.724 --> 00:04:40.940
(dragon roaring)

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:43.710
Katcher: That was the design
I did for the ice dragon,

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:46.420
which I had to keep secret for two years

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:47.470
before it was ever released.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:50.030
Even my wife did not see that. (laughs)

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:51.967
Because everybody was like,
especially my wife was like,

00:04:51.967 --> 00:04:53.464
"Don't you tell me what happens,

00:04:53.464 --> 00:04:54.989
don't show me anything on your computer,

00:04:54.989 --> 00:04:56.947
I don't want to know
until the show comes out."

